|\n| **TOTAL** | **100%** | |\n\n**Step 1-3. VIK Macro Sensitivity Matrix**\n\n| Sensitivity Axis | Score (\u22123 to +3) | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Interest Rate Sensitivity | \u22121 | 1.1x net leverage; cash cushion $3.8B; medium duration consumer (affluent, not mortgage-dependent) |\n| Dollar Sensitivity | \u22122 | ~30\u201340% of vessel/cost base EUR-denominated [Assumption]; weak dollar is a COST HEADWIND. Revenue is USD-priced. |\n| Liquidity Sensitivity | +2 | Customer-deposit float ($4.6B deferred revenue); undrawn revolver $1.0B; strong OCF |\n| Economic Cycle Sensitivity | \u22123 | Luxury discretionary travel; 2008/2020 demand collapses validate cyclical beta 1.5 |\n| Commodity Sensitivity | \u22122 | Fuel is a direct expense; not fully hedged at all times |\n\n**Step 1-4. Macro Alignment Judgment**\nRegime-weighted expected impact: **MILD HEADWIND** (\u22120.2 on composite). Reflation helps bookings; stagflation/rates hurts multiple; weak dollar hurts EUR-denominated costs. **Conviction \u22120.5 steps.** Strengthen split-entry discipline and require wider margin of safety than in a Goldilocks world.\n\n---\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\n**Step 1.5-1. Base Rates (Public Research/Long-History)**\n\n| Question | Base Rate | Source/Justification |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5-year survival, established luxury cruise operator | ~90% | Cash-generative, 1.1x net leverage, $3.8B cash + $1.0B revolver [Actual] |\n| Sustaining >15% revenue CAGR for 5 consecutive years (leisure/travel operator) | ~15\u201325% | Travel industry reversion to ~5\u20137% long-run demand growth; capacity competition |\n| Recession (NBER-defined) within 3 years | ~30\u201340% | Historical frequency; currently late expansion with 3.4% CPI and Fed on hold |\n| Cyclical peak to drawdown >40% (expedia/royal/carnival history) | ~30\u201340% | 2008, 2020 precedents |\n| Platform/network transition success (N/A \u2014 pure asset player) | N/A | \u2014 |\n\n**Step 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rates**\nMy base case assumes 9\u201313% revenue growth for 4\u20135 years, then 5\u20136%. This is above the 15\u201325% historical achievability base for >15% growth but BELOW the pure base-rate reversion. Justifications:\n1. **Forward-sold visibility**: 86% of FY2026 capacity already booked as of Feb 2026 [Actual]; 38% of FY2027 pre-booked. This is not a hockey-stick narrative; it is contracted demand.\n2. **Capacity under contract**: 27 river ships by 2028, 10 ocean ships by 2031 [Estimate \u2014 GuruFocus citing company]; orderbook visibility is unusually high.\n3. **Moat economics**: 60%+ repeat guest rate [Estimate \u2014 research brief]; 45.8% ROIC [Actual]; negative working-capital float [$1.2B negative WC \u2014 Actual].\n**Verdict**: Deviation from pessimistic base rate has at least 3 specific justifications. No penalty.\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check**\nBull-case CAGR > 1.5\u00d7 base-rate achievability \u2192 **REQUIRES ADDITIONAL EVIDENCE**. Bull case assumes 14\u201316% CAGR through 2030. This exceeds base rates by >2\u00d7. **RED FLAG.** Only rescue evidence would be proof of new large addressable markets (China outbound, ocean-fleet share gains). Current China itineraries are pilot-stage, not proven. **Bull case probability capped at 25%.**\n\n---\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY (Buffett + Lynch Framework)\n\n**Step 2-1. Business Understanding**\nViking designs, markets, and operates premium river, ocean, and expedition cruise ships for affluent (55+) English-speaking travelers, selling directly to consumers rather than through agencies. It monetizes destination immersion at 95% occupancy with a 60% repeat-guest base. Understandability: **HIGH**.\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat Assessment + Moat Dynamics**\n\n| Moat Type | Score (0\u20133) | Notes | Direction |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Intangible Assets (brand/patents) | 3 | Cond\u00e9 Nast/Travel+Leisure #1; brand = permission to price at premium | Widening |\n| Switching Costs | 2 | Repeat guests 60%; loyalty, but no contractual lock-in | Stable |\n| Network Effects | 1 | Destination network breadth, not classic 2-sided network | Stable |\n| Cost Advantage | 3 | DTC distribution; scale in 100-vessel fleet, shipbuilding contracts | Widening |\n| Economies of Scale | 2 | Fleet scale in ship management, negotiating, marketing | Widening |\n| Regulatory Barriers | 2 | Port access, safety, certification for 100+ ships | Stable |\n| Data Moat | 2 | 30 years of guest preference/booking data; yields/pricing | Widening |\n| **TOTAL** | **15/21** | **WIDE MOAT** | **WIDENING** |\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch Classification**\n**FAST GROWER** with cyclical characteristics. Revenue CAGR FY22\u201325: ~32% [Arithmetic \u2014 3.176\u21926.501]. Lynch checkpoints: \"Follow the growth, don't overpay.\" Warning: P/E 36\u00d7 trailing, 30\u00d7 forward \u2014 overpay risk is exactly the problem. Also classifyable as \"Cyclical\" if 2008/2020 repeats.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard**\n\n| Metric | FY2025 | FY2024 | FY2023 | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue Growth (YoY) | +21.9% [Actual] | +13.2% [Arithmetic] | +48.3% [Arithmetic] | Excellent |\n| Operating Margin | 23.1% [Actual] | 20.2% [Actual] | 17.3% [Actual] | Good, improving |\n| Net Income | $1.148B [Actual] | $0.153B [Actual] | \u2212$1.850B [Actual] | Recovering strongly |\n| ROE (company basis) | ~100%+ (tiny equity) | N/M | N/M | Not meaningful |\n| ROIC (company-reported) | 45.8% [Actual] | \u2014 | \u2014 | Exceptional |\n| FCF Conversion | OCF $2.56B [Actual]; FCF \u2248 $1.6B [Estimate] | \u2014 | \u2014 | Strong |\n| Net Debt / EBITDA | 1.1\u00d7 [Actual \u2014 company] | 2.4\u00d7 | 3.5\u00d7+ | Excellent |\n| Interest Coverage (OII/interest) | 4.1\u00d7 [Arithmetic: 1,502/363] | 2.8\u00d7 | 1.5\u00d7 | Adequate\u2192Good |\n| Net Working Capital | \u2212$1.214B [Actual] | \u2212$2.004B | \u2212$2.011B | Structural float advantage |\n\nOverall: **EXCELLENT** \u2014 improving margins, fortress liquidity, float model, low leverage post-IPO.\n\n**Step 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard**\n\n| Criterion | Score (0\u20133) | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback Efficiency | 0 | No buybacks; secondary float building. 0 pts |\n| M&A Track Record | 2 | Small targeted add-ons (shipyard commitments, no large acquisitions); no impairments |\n| Capex Efficiency | 3 | 45.8% ROIC [Actual] on newbuild program. Incremental ROIC likely >30% |\n| Rationality of Mix | 3 | Ship capex tightly matched to 86% pre-booked demand; disciplined |\n| Guidance Accuracy | 3 | Q2 2026 EPS [$1.31 vs $1.26 est \u2014 Estimate] and revenue consistently beating despite Q1 seasonal loss |\n| **Subtotal** | **11/15** | |\n| Insider ownership bonus | \u22122 | Management stake 0.79% [Actual \u2014 vendor], no recent 6-mo net insider buying [Unconfirmed] |\n\n**Capital allocation: GOOD.** Scale-to-demand discipline (only building where bookings justify) is a competitive advantage.\n\n---\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\n**Step 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization**\nN/A \u2014 no R&D line for a cruise ship operator.\n\n**Step 2.5-2. SBC Treatment**\nSBC is deducted from FCF throughout this analysis: FY2025 $89M [Actual], projected to $200M by 2035 [Assumption]. Adjusted FCF = OCF \u2212 SBC \u2212 Capex.\n\n**Step 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization**\nOperating leases $239M (2025) [Actual]; small vs $12.2B assets. We add to total debt +$0.239B for conservatism in net-debt computation; the data pack warns the LT-debt and lease columns may overlap, so I split the difference: use **$5.502B debt (LT + current portion) and optionally note lease-adjusted $5.741B = exactly the vendor total-debt figure, which we treat as coincidence not confirmation [Unconfirmed]**.\n\n**Step 2.5-4. Brand/Data Asset Estimation**\nBrand: #1 in river/ocean/expedition (Cond\u00e9 Nast/T+L). Interbrand-style value not computable from pack; assume brand contributes ~10\u201315% of FCF durability [Assumption]. Data: 30 years of guest-level purchase and itinerary data; monetized through yield management.\n\n**Step 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC**\nAdjusted ROIC = 45.8% [Actual \u2014 company-calculated]. GAAP pre-tax ROIC = NOPAT / (Debt + Equity \u2212 Cash) = [$1,502\u00d70.88] / [$5.502+$1.121\u2212$3.804] = $1,322 / $2,819 = 46.9% [Estimate]. Gap is negligible; no major distortion beyond SBC treatment (which we deduct).\n\n**Step 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (2025)**\nOwner Earnings = NI + D&A \u2212 Maintenance Capex \u2212 \u0394WC = $1,148M + $285M \u2212 $350M [Assumption] \u2212 $790M [Actual] = **$293M**. But note: \u0394WC = +$790M driven by advance-booking float growth \u2014 that is *growth investment*, not pure maintenance. Excluding growth-driven float: Steady-state OE \u2248 $1,083M.\n\n---\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 1 \u2014 TRADITIONAL DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\n**Step 0-1/0-2/0-3/0-4. Archetype Determination & Model Selection**\n\n- **Archetype: B \u2014 Quality Compounder** (High ROIC 45.8%, steady +21.9% growth, wide moat, negative-WC float, fortress balance sheet).\n- Why not Cyclical (C)?: Although discretionary travel is cyclical, Viking's 3-year-advance-booking float and 100-vessel committed capacity create visibility that materially de-risks the near-term cycle. It behaves like a quality compounder with cyclical tail-risk.\n- Why not Engine 4 (Option)?: Core products are 100% of revenue; no optionality-dominated story.\n- Transition status: Not in transition.\n- Engine selection: **Engine 1 (DCF + Multiples)**.\n- If I had wrongly applied Engine 2 (Cyclical), I would mid-cycle earnings erroneously and discount Viking's structural ROIC/float advantage.\n\n---\n\n**3.1 FORECAST PATH \u2014 BASE CASE (10-Year Explicit Stage 1, 2026\u20132035)**\n\n**Sources**: FY2025 actuals from filings/press release [Actual]; FY2026 revenue guidance $7.367B [Estimate \u2014 aggregator; consistent with 86% booked & +7% capacity]; growth 2027\u20132031 from fleet orderbook (27 river by 2028, 10 ocean by 2031) [Assumption]; 2032\u20132035 fade toward low-single-digit as orderbook completes [Assumption]. Margin path: OPM 23.1% \u2192 26% by 2032 on operating leverage, yield +7.4%/yr and pricing discipline [Assumption]. Capex: $850M 2025 [Estimate], $1.1\u20131.2B/yr 2026\u201328 (10 river + 2 ocean delivered 2026; continued builds) [Assumption], $800\u2013950M 2029\u201331 [Assumption], $450\u2013500M 2032+ maintenance/drydock only [Assumption]. D&A grows from $285M to $660M [Assumption]. Tax rate 12% Bermuda structure [Assumption]. Interest expense: $350M\u2192$530M [Assumption]; interest income: $150M\u2192$220M [Assumption].\n\n**Explicit Forecast \u2014 Base Case**\n\n| FY | Revenue ($M) | Rev Growth | OI Margin | OI ($M) | D&A ($M) | Net Cash (Int, Inc \u2212 Exp) | Pre-tax ($M) | Tax 12% | NI ($M) | \u0394WC ($M) | Capex ($M) | SBC ($M) | FCF ($M) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2025A | 6,501 | +21.9% | 23.1% | 1,502 | 285 | \u2212213 | 1,289 | 141 | 1,148 | 790 | 850 | 89 | 1,621 |\n| 2026E | 7,367 | +13.3% | 23.5% | 1,732 | 300 | \u2212200 | 1,532 | 184 | 1,348 | 450 | 1,200 | 120 | 778 |\n| 2027E | 8,325 | +13.0% | 24.0% | 2,000 | 340 | \u2212220 | 1,780 | 214 | 1,566 | 500 | 1,200 | 130 | 1,076 |\n| 2028E | 9,325 | +12.0% | 24.8% | 2,314 | 380 | \u2212230 | 2,084 | 250 | 1,834 | 550 | 1,100 | 140 | 1,524 |\n| 2029E | 10,260 | +10.0% | 25.0% | 2,565 | 430 | \u2212240 | 2,325 | 279 | 2,046 | 600 | 950 | 150 | 1,976 |\n| 2030E | 11,170 | +9.0% | 25.0% | 2,792 | 480 | \u2212260 | 2,532 | 304 | 2,228 | 650 | 850 | 160 | 2,348 |\n| 2031E | 12,175 | +9.0% | 25.5% | 3,110 | 520 | \u2212280 | 2,830 | 340 | 2,490 | 700 | 800 | 170 | 2,740 |\n| 2032E | 13,150 | +8.0% | 26.0% | 3,406 | 560 | \u2212290 | 3,116 | 374 | 2,742 | 700 | 500 | 180 | 3,322 |\n| 2033E | 13,940 | +6.0% | 26.0% | 3,614 | 600 | \u2212300 | 3,314 | 398 | 2,916 | 600 | 450 | 190 | 3,476 |\n| 2034E | 14,640 | +5.0% | 26.0% | 3,796 | 630 | \u2212310 | 3,486 | 418 | 3,068 | 600 | 450 | 200 | 3,648 |\n| 2035E | 15,370 | +5.0% | 26.0% | 3,978 | 660 | \u2212310 | 3,668 | 440 | 3,228 | 600 | 450 | 200 | 3,838 |\n\n*Note: 2025A FCF of $1,621M includes one-time-ish \u0394WC (+$790M) and excludes interest income/expense as modeled for future years; the projected FCFs derive from NI + D&A + \u0394WC \u2212 Capex \u2212 SBC, which is internally consistent with my OCF proxy.*\n\n**3.2 Discount-Rate Derivation**\n\n| Component | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Risk-free rate | 4.71% | [Actual \u2014 10Y UST, Aug 21, 2026] |\n| Equity risk premium | 4.5% | [Assumption \u2014 Damodaran-style long-run] |\n| Beta | 1.2 | [Assumption \u2014 vendor states 1.503 [Estimate]; I set 1.2 for fortress balance sheet, float model, visible book; acknowledged judgment call] |\n| Cost of equity | 4.71 + 1.2 \u00d7 4.5 = 10.1% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Cost of debt pre-tax | 5.8% (4.71 + ~110bps, BB profile with investment-grade leverage at 1.1x) | [Assumption] |\n| Tax rate | 12% | [Assumption \u2014 Bermuda holding structure] |\n| After-tax cost of debt | 5.1% | [Arithmetic] |\n| Weights: E / D | 88.1% / 11.9% | [Arithmetic \u2014 market cap $40.9B, net debt $1.7B] |\n| **WACC** | **0.881\u00d710.1 + 0.119\u00d75.1 = 9.5%** | [Arithmetic] |\n| Terminal growth | 2.5% | [Assumption \u2014 below nominal GDP 3.5%] |\n| 10-Year stage, then Gordon TV | \u2014 | Consistent with 2036+ orderbook completion |\n\n**3.3 DCF Output \u2014 Base Case**\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| PV of 2026\u20132035 FCFs | $13,825M |\n| Terminal Value at end-2035 | $56,200M |\n| PV of Terminal Value | $22,677M |\n| Enterprise Value | $36,502M |\n| Less: Net Debt ($5,502 \u2212 $3,804) | $1,698M |\n| Equity Value | $34,804M |\n| Shares Outstanding | 440.6M |\n| **Intrinsic Value per Share (Base)** | **$79.00** |\n\n**3.4 Multiples Cross-Check**\n\n- Trailing P/E: $92.79 / $2.57 = 36.1x [Actual]. Quality-compounder 10-year P/E average: 25\u201328x for best-in-class; 36x is a premium multiple only justified by 15%+ sustained EPS growth.\n- Forward P/E (FY2026E EPS ~$3.05): 30.4x [Assumption on EPS]. RCL trades near 17\u201320x, CCL 12\u201315x [Estimate \u2014 general market knowledge, not independently searched]. Viking's moat and float justify a premium, but 30x forward is 1.5\u20132.0x the sector.\n- EV/EBITDA: EV $42.6B / Adj. EBITDA $1.872B = 22.8x [Arithmetic]. Sector range 8\u201312x [Estimate]. Only defensible if EBITDA grows >20%/yr for 5+ years.\n- **Multiples verdict**: Current price embeds premium multiple AND flawless execution. No margin of safety.\n\n**3.5 Reverse DCF / Market-Implied Expectations**\nAt $92.79 (EV $42.6B), WACC 9.5%, g 2.5%, implied steady-sustainable FCF = (42.6 \u00d7 0.07) / 1.025 = **$2.91B/yr from 2035 onward** \u2014 requires ~31% FCF margin by 2035, a level no cruise operator has ever sustained at a fleet of 140+ ships. **Feasible in steady state after all newbuild capex ends; the issue is that the market pays for this TODAY, with no cyclicality discount.**\n\n**3.6 Tornado Sensitivity (\u00b11\u03c3 on key drivers)**\n\n| Variable | \u22121\u03c3 | Base | +1\u03c3 | Impact on IV |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| OI Margin 2030 | 22% | 25% | 28% | $64 \u2192 $79 \u2192 $94 |\n| Revenue CAGR 26\u201330 | 8% | 11.6% | 14% | $69 \u2192 $79 \u2192 $90 |\n| WACC | 10.5% | 9.5% | 8.5% | $70 \u2192 $79 \u2192 $95 |\n| Terminal growth | 2.0% | 2.5% | 3.0% | $76 \u2192 $79 \u2192 $84 |\n| 2026\u201328 Capex (avg/yr) | $1,300M | $1,167M | $1,000M | $73 \u2192 $79 \u2192 $85 |\n\n**Top 3 Thesis KPIs: (1) FY2027 occupancy/yield, (2) Operating margin trajectory to 25% by 2029, (3) Delivery of 27 river ships on schedule without building ahead of demand.**\n\n---\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n**Step 3.5-1. Competitive Response**\nRiver-cruise capacity expansion (A-ROSA, CroisiEurope, Tauck, Uniworld, Scenic, Emerald, AmaWaterways) is aggressive. If Viking's 27-ship river orderbook (2026\u201328) lands on a European river system with competitors adding similar counts, **capacity oversupply is the #2 bear risk** (after macro). Pricing power (-2 on the \u22123/+3 scale) for the industry if supply races demand; Viking's brand halo may protect it better than smaller operators. Ocean segment: RCL's Celebrity and MSC are pushing upscale; Viking's adult-only, enrichment positioning remains differentiated but competition is growing.\n\n**Step 3.5-2. Reflexivity**\nPositive: Strong stock price \u2192 SBC is attractive talent currency, access to ship-financing, and brand trust for elderly affluent travelers. Negative: A drawdown below $56 would dent consumer confidence in a *travel luxury* product that is prepaid 12\u201318 months in advance. Reflexivity risk is moderate-to-high.\n\n**Step 3.5-3. Customer Behavior Evolution**\nBoomers are retiring at ~10,000/day in the US [Estimate]. Viking's core demographic (65+) is growing; as early adopters age out, new-to-brand retirees are entering. If enthusiasm for experiential travel continues, 60% repeat rate holds; LTV/CAC should improve with direct-to-consumer brand marketing. If post-retirement budgets compress (healthcare inflation), yields soften. The \"Silver Dollar\" trend is supportive for 5\u201310 years.\n\n---\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE (BAYESIAN)\n\n**Step 4-1/2/4. Scenario Probabilities (TOTAL = 100%)**\n\n| Scenario | Probability | Per-share Value | Justification |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bear \u2014 Cyclical Recession + Capacity Glut** | 25% | $23 | 2027 recession (15% regime) or river oversupply cuts yields. Occupancy 85%, OI margin troughs at 15%. Revenue <FY2025 base through 2028. 5-year survival still high, but equity multiple re-rates to 12\u201315x trough earnings. |\n| **Base \u2014 Smooth Compound** | 50% | $79 | 2026 guidance met; 9\u201313% growth to 2031, fade to 5%; OI margin to 25\u201326%; capex peaks 2026\u201328 then falls; WACC 9.5%. |\n| **Bull \u2014 Chi