th staged entry rather than full position.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\n**Step 1.5-1. Base Rate Inquiry**\n\n| Question | Base Rate | Applied to VIK |\n|---|---|---|\n| Probability a Quality Compounder survives 5 years | >90% | VIK: profitable, $3.8B cash, investment-grade trajectory \u2014 **95%** |\n| Probability a luxury cruise brand sustains double-digit revenue growth for 5 years | ~30-40% | History: FY22-25 revenue CAGR 27%; 2026/27 bookings strong \u2014 **60%** |\n| Probability large cruise capacity additions cause yield compression | ~50-60% | Global order book: 64 ships / $40B+ capex 2026-28 [Estimate \u2014 Hope Research] \u2014 **55%** |\n| Probability M&A achieves ROIC>WACC | N/A | Viking grows organically \u2014 not applicable |\n| Recent IPO underperformance base rate | ~60% of IPOs underperform in 3 years | 2 years post-IPO; shares up ~65% from lows \u2014 partly deviates |\n\n**Step 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate \u2014 Justifications**\n\nTo deviate upward from the ~35% base rate for sustained double-digit growth, I cite:\n1. **92% of 2026 capacity already booked**, 38% of 2027 booked at rates +12% YoY [Actual \u2014 Q1 2026 call].\n2. **60%+ repeat guest rate** (research brief, [Estimate]) \u2014 structurally de-risks demand.\n3. **Negative working capital model** \u2014 customers prepay; deferred revenue $5.4B [Actual \u2014 Q1 call].\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check**\n\nMy central success probability (~60%) is **1.7x** the base rate (35%). This triggers the 1.5x threshold \u2014 requires additional evidence. Supporting evidence: Q1 2026 adjusted EBITDA +43.9% YoY [Actual], yield +9.5% [Actual], capacity +15% in 2027 [Actual]. This satisfies the additional-evidence requirement but caps central-case probability at 60%.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY ASSESSMENT\n\n**Step 2-1. Business Understanding**\n\nViking is the world's largest river cruise operator and a growing premium ocean cruise line, operating ~96 vessels at ~95% occupancy. It sells direct-to-consumer to affluent 55+ travelers, achieving 60%+ repeat rates and revenue of $6.50B in FY2025. The model generates negative working capital via customer prepayments.\n\n**Understandability Rating: High.**\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat Assessment + Dynamics**\n\n| Moat Type | Score (0-3) | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible Assets (Brand) | **3** \u2014 Viking = category king in river cruising | **Widening** \u2014 Time's Most Influential Companies, 2026 |\n| Switching Costs | **2** \u2014 60% repeat rate, but no contractual lock | **Stable** |\n| Network Effects | **1** \u2014 Repeat-guest community, not true network effects | **Stable** |\n| Cost Advantage | **2** \u2014 DTC model saves agent commissions; standardized ship classes | **Stable** |\n| Economies of Scale | **2** \u2014 96 vessels; procurement scale | **Widening** \u2014 fleet growth |\n| Regulatory Barriers | **1** \u2014 Flag/port access, but not exclusive | **Stable** |\n| Data Moat | **1** \u2014 Yield management via guest data | **Widening** \u2014 personalization |\n| **Total** | **12/21 \u2014 Wide Moat** | **Stable-to-Widening** |\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch Classification**\n\n**Fast Grower** \u2014 Revenue CAGR FY22-25 = 27% [Arithmetic], Q1 2026 revenue +17.5% [Actual].\nCheckpoints: (a) growth duration \u2014 92% booked 2026, 38% booked 2027 [Actual]; (b) balance sheet \u2014 net debt ~$1.9B [Actual-Q1 call], manageable; (c) P/E vs growth \u2014 36x trailing / ~28x forward vs 32% EPS growth = PEG ~0.87 [Arithmetic] \u2014 acceptable if growth persists.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard**\n\n| Metric | FY2025 [Actual] | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue Growth (3-yr CAGR) | **27.0%** [Arithmetic] | Excellent |\n| Operating Margin | **23.1%** latest; 18.7% median [Arithmetic] | Excellent \u2014 rising trend |\n| Net Income | **$1.148B** [Actual] | Positive post-IPO; FY23 loss was transition/one-off [Estimate] |\n| ROE | 3.0% (vendor,