 is the world's largest river cruise operator and a growing premium ocean cruise line, operating ~96 vessels at ~95% occupancy. It sells direct-to-consumer to affluent 55+ travelers, achieving 60%+ repeat rates and revenue of $6.50B in FY2025. The model generates negative working capital via customer prepayments.\n\n**Understandability Rating: High.**\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat Assessment + Dynamics**\n\n| Moat Type | Score (0-3) | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible Assets (Brand) | **3** \u2014 Viking = category king in river cruising | **Widening** \u2014 Time's Most Influential Companies, 2026 |\n| Switching Costs | **2** \u2014 60% repeat rate, but no contractual lock | **Stable** |\n| Network Effects | **1** \u2014 Repeat-guest community, not true network effects | **Stable** |\n| Cost Advantage | **2** \u2014 DTC model saves agent commissions; standardized ship classes | **Stable** |\n| Economies of Scale | **2** \u2014 96 vessels; procurement scale | **Widening** \u2014 fleet growth |\n| Regulatory Barriers | **1** \u2014 Flag/port access, but not exclusive | **Stable** |\n| Data Moat | **1** \u2014 Yield management via guest data | **Widening** \u2014 personalization |\n| **Total** | **12/21 \u2014 Wide Moat** | **Stable-to-Widening** |\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch Classification**\n\n**Fast Grower** \u2014 Revenue CAGR FY22-25 = 27% [Arithmetic], Q1 2026 revenue +17.5% [Actual].\nCheckpoints: (a) growth duration \u2014 92% booked 2026, 38% booked 2027 [Actual]; (b) balance sheet \u2014 net debt ~$1.9B [Actual-Q1 call], manageable; (c) P/E vs growth \u2014 36x trailing / ~28x forward vs 32% EPS growth = PEG ~0.87 [Arithmetic] \u2014 acceptable if growth persists.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard**\n\n| Metric | FY2025 [Actual] | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue Growth (3-yr CAGR) | **27.0%** [Arithmetic] | Excellent |\n| Operating Margin | **23.1%** latest; 18.7% median [Arithmetic] | Excellent \u2014 rising trend |\n| Net Income | **$1.148B** [Actual] | Positive post-IPO; FY23 loss was transition/one-off [Estimate] |\n| ROE | 3.0% (vendor, distorted by negative historical equity) | Inadequate measure here |\n| FCF Margin | Not directly available; OCF margin 39.4% [Arithmetic] | Strong |\n| Net Debt/EBITDA | Net debt ~$1.9B / EBITDA $1.79B = **~1.1x** [Arithmetic] | Strong |\n| Interest Coverage | OI $1.502B / Interest $0.363B = **4.1x** [Arithmetic] | Adequate |\n\n**Judgment: Excellent** \u2014 with caveat that FY2023's $1.85B net loss remains unexplained by our data (likely one-off/IPO-related) [Unconfirmed].\n\n**Step 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard**\n\n| Criterion | Score | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency | 0/3 | No buybacks; reinvesting in fleet \u2014 appropriate at this stage |\n| M&A track record | 2/3 | Organic; targeted ship acquisitions (Viking Yidun) |\n| Capex efficiency | 2/3 | New ships targeting high ROIC; 2027 capacity +15% pre-sold 38% |\n| Rationality of mix | 2/3 | Clear reinvest-first policy stated on Q1 call [Actual] |\n| Guidance accuracy | 2/3 | Q2 2026 EPS beat consensus by $0.36 [Estimate \u2014 Investing.com] |\n| **Total** | **8/15** | |\n\n**Insider ownership bonus:** 0 (insider ownership 0.79% per vendor; founder likely holds via entities [Unconfirmed]).\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\n**Step 2.5-1. R&D Capitalization:** R&D \u2248 $0 \u2014 not applicable.\n\n**Step 2.5-2. SBC Treatment:**\n\n| FY | SBC [Actual] | OCF [Actual] | SBC/OCF |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2022 | $0.025B | $0.373B | 6.7% |\n| 2023 | $0.018B | $1.371B | 1.3% |\n| 2024 | $0.014B | $2.082B | 0.7% |\n| 2025 | $0.089B | $2.560B | 3.5% |\n\nSBC is modest. **Adjusted FCF = OCF \u2212 SBC \u2212 Capex.** All my FCF projections deduct SBC explicitly.\n\n**Step 2.5-3. Operating Lease Capitalization:** FY2025 leases $0.239B [Actual]. Capitalized at 8x = **$1.9B** additional debt [Assumption]. Adjusted net debt = $5.13B LT + $0.38B current + $1.9B leases \u2212 $3.80B cash = **$3.6B**. Elevated, but covered by EBIT of $1.5B.\n\n**Step 2.5-4. Brand/Data Assets:** Not quantified. Qualitative: brand is Viking's core asset; reflected in DTC pricing power.\n\n**Step 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC:**\n\n- NOPAT 2025 = OI $1.502B \u00d7 (1\u22120.15) = **$1.28B** [Arithmetic \u2014 tax rate assumption 15%]\n- Invested capital = Equity $1.12B + Debt $5.50B + Leases $1.9B = **$8.52B**\n- **Adjusted ROIC = 15.0%** vs WACC 9.8% \u2014 spread of ~5.2pts.\n\n**Step 2.5-6. Owner Earnings (Buffett):**\n\nOwner Earnings 2025 = NI $1.148B + D&A $0.285B \u2212 Maintenance capex [Assumption $0.40B] \u2212 \u0394WC $0.79B (cash use) = **$0.24B**. This is depressed by the 2025 working capital swing (deferred revenue normalization). On a normalized basis (\u0394WC=0): **$1.03B** [Estimate].\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 1 \u2014 DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\n### 3-1. Discount Rate Derivation\n\n| Input | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Risk-free rate | **4.70%** | [Actual \u2014 Fed H.15, 10Y Treasury 8/24/26] |\n| Beta | **1.20** | Vendor beta 1.503; adjusted down for Viking's repeat-rate/recession-resilient model [Assumption] |\n| Equity risk premium | **4.80%** | [Assumption \u2014 long-term average] |\n| Cost of equity | 4.70 + 1.20\u00d74.80 = **10.46%** | [Arithmetic] |\n| Pre-tax cost of debt | **5.80%** | 10Y + 110bps spread [Assumption \u2014 implied by BB+/Ba1 profile] |\n| Tax rate | **15%** | Management effective tax [Assumption] |\n| After-tax cost of debt | **4.93%** | [Arithmetic] |\n| Equity weight | **88%** | Mkt cap $40.9B / (EV $42.6B + leases adj.) [Arithmetic] |\n| Debt weight | **12%** | Net debt $1.7B (ex-lease) [Arithmetic] |\n| **WACC** | **= 0.88\u00d710.46% + 0.12\u00d74.93% = 9.80%** | [Arithmetic] |\n\n### 3-2. Explicit Forecast (2026\u20132031)\n\n| Line ($B) | 2025A | 2026E | 2027E | 2028E | 2029E | 2030E | 2031E |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | 6.501 | 7.64 | 8.64 | 9.50 | 10.26 | 10.87 | 11.41 |\n| Growth % | +21.9% | +17.5% | +13.0% | +10.0% | +8.0% | +6.0% | +5.0% |\n| OCF margin % | 39.4% | 40.0% | 40.0% | 40.0% | 40.0% | 40.0% | 40.0% |\n| OCF | 2.560 | 3.06 | 3.46 | 3.80 | 4.10 | 4.35 | 4.56 |\n| Capex (gross) | n/a | (1.90) | (1.00) | (0.90) | (0.75) | (0.70) | (0.70) |\n| SBC | (0.089) | (0.10) | (0.12) | (0.13) | (0.13) | (0.14) | (0.15) |\n| **FCF** | \u2014 | **1.06** | **2.34** | **2.77** | **3.22** | **3.51** | **3.71** |\n\n**Source trace for every driver:**\n- **2026 revenue +17.5%**: [Actual \u2014 Q1 2026 revenue +17.5% YoY; research brief cites FY guidance $7.367B = +13.3% (more conservative). I use the higher 17.5% because Q1 beat and consensus forward growth = +19.8% [Estimate \u2014 yfinance]. Blended judgment: **$7.64B** [Estimate].\n- **2027 +13%**: [Actual driver \u2014 capacity +15% for core product, 38% pre-booked at +12% rates (Q1 call)]. Growth 13% reflects capacity mix and revenue per PCD [Estimate].\n- **2028-2031 10%\u21925%**: [Assumption \u2014 decelerates to industry capacity growth ~6.2% CAGR [Estimate \u2014 Hope Research]; company-specific premium fades after 2027 as newbuild program completes. No company guidance beyond 2027 [Unconfirmed].\n- **OCF margin 40%**: [Arithmetic anchor \u2014 FY2025 actual 39.4%; slight expansion from pricing power. Alternative range 37-42% shown in sensitivity below].\n- **Capex 2026 $1.9B / 2027 $1.0B**: [Actual \u2014 Q1 call: \"Total committed ship CapEx for 2026 is about $1.9B... for 2027, $1B\"]. 2028-31: [Assumption \u2014 $0.7-0.9B, reflecting 2 Egypt ships in 2028 then maintenance].\n- **SBC**: [Estimate \u2014 trend from filed SBC; $0.089B in 2025].\n\n### 3-3. Terminal Value\n\n- Terminal FCF 2032 = $3.71B \u00d7 1.03 = **$3.82B** [Assumption \u2014 3% nominal GDP growth]\n- Terminal ROIC = 15% (above WACC) \u2192 reinvestment = 3%/15% = 20%; terminal FCF already reflects this.\n- **TV = $3.82 / (9.80% \u2212 3.0%) = $56.2B**\n\n### 3-4. DCF Math\n\n| Year | FCF | Discount Factor @9.8% | PV |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026 | 1.06 | 0.911 | 0.97 |\n| 2027 | 2.34 | 0.830 | 1.94 |\n| 2028 | 2.77 | 0.756 | 2.09 |\n| 2029 | 3.22 | 0.688 | 2.22 |\n| 2030 | 3.51 | 0.627 | 2.20 |\n| TV | 56.2 | 0.627 | 35.25 |\n| **EV** | | | **44.67** |\n\n**Equity bridge:**\nEV $44.67B\n+ Cash $3.80B [Actual FY2025; Q1 2026 $4.0B]\n\u2212 LT Debt $5.13B \u2212 Current portion $0.38B [Actual \u2014 6B table]\n\u2212 Capitalized leases $1.9B [Assumption]\n**= Equity $37.06B** (ex-lease) / **$35.16B** (lease-adjusted)\n\n**Per share (440.6M):**\n- Ex-lease: **$84.1/share**\n- Lease-adjusted: **$79.8/share**\n\nThis is *below* market price. However, my central-case DCF above uses the conservative gross-capex method, which is correct for FCFF. The gap indicates the market is pricing either higher growth or lower WACC. Let me show the cross-check.\n\n### 3-5. Reverse DCF \u2014 What Does $92.79 Imply?\n\nAt $92.79, EV = $92.79\u00d7440.6M + $5.51B debt + $1.9B leases \u2212 $3.8B cash = $44.5B. For WACC 9.8% and g 3%, required terminal FCF 2032 = PV(TV) at EV = (44.5 \u2212 \u03a3PV FCF 2026-30). With my central FCF stream, required TV = $54.2B \u2192 implies 2032 FCF = $3.68B \u2192 implies 2031 revenue at 40% OCF margin = $11.4B and FCF conversion \u2248 my central case. **Conclusion: $92.79 \u2248 my central case with leases included.** The market is pricing the central case precisely.\n\n### 3-6. Multiples Cross-Check\n\n| Multiple | VIK | Peers (RCL/CCL/NCLH typical) | Assessment |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Trailing P/E | 36.4x | 12-15x | Premium justified by growth (27% rev CAGR) but rich |\n| Forward P/E (2026E) | ~29x | ~10-13x | Rich \u2014 requires continued 30% EPS growth |\n| EV/EBITDA | 23.8x (FY25) | ~10-12x | Rich |\n| PEG (trailing) | ~0.87 | ~1.5 | Fair-to-cheap *if* 32% EPS growth persists |\n\nMultiples alone: **no margin of safety.** Multiples say \"expense\"; DCF says \"fairly valued.\"\n\n### 3-7. Tornado Sensitivity (Top 3 Variables)\n\n| Variable | -1\u03c3 Effect on IV | +1\u03c3 Effect on IV |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2031 revenue growth path | -$22 (if 2028-31 avg 3%, not 7%) | +$18 (if avg 10%) |\n| OCF margin (40% central; 37%-42% range) | -$11 | +$12 |\n| WACC (9.8% central; 9.0%-10.6%) | +$15 | -$13 |\n\nThesis KPIs = these three.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n**Step 3.5-1. Competitive Response:**\n- Rivals (RCL, CCL, Avalon, Uniworld) are ordering aggressively. Global order book: 64 ships, >$40B [Estimate \u2014 Hope Research]. Expected industry capacity +6.2% CAGR 2026-2028. **Response: price pressure on low-end; Viking's high-repeat/affluent niche is partly insulated. Impact: -1 to +1.**\n\n**Step 3.5-2. Reflexivity:**\n- Positive loop: Strong share price \u2192 SBC attracts talent (not a major factor at 3.5% of OCF), but more importantly, high valuation \u2192 cheap equity/accretion for future fleet funding **if they issue shares** (they have not).\n- Negative loop: Weak travel demand \u2192 stock drop \u2192 higher WACC \u2192 costly financing for 2028 newbuilds. Moderate risk.\n\n**Step 3.5-3. Customer Behavior Evolution:**\n- Viking's core guest is 55+ affluent. As this cohort grows (boomer retirement), demand tailwind is secular. But competition will chase the same demographic. LTV/CAC: repeat guest rate 60%+ reduces CAC; rising marketing (SG&A +16.6% in 2025) shows they are paying more to reach new guests. **Watch SG&A growth vs bookings growth.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE\n\n**Step 4-1/2. Scenario Composition**\n\n| Scenario | Probability | Core Drivers | Target Price (3-yr) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bear \u2014 Recession / Oversupply** | **20%** | Recession hits 2027; affluent travelers defer; industry oversupply forces yield cut; 2027 bookings (38% on the books) cancel; net yield -8%; revenue 2027 -5%; FCF 2026-28 negative cumulative; recovery slow. | **$55** (\u221241%) |\n| **Base \u2014 Steady Compounding** | **50%** | 2026 +17.5%; 2027 +13%; 2028-31 +10%\u21925%; OCF margin 40%; net yield +4-6%/yr; capex per committed schedule; no recession. | **$98** (+6%) |\n| **Bull \u2014 Share Gains + China Delta** | **30%** | 2027 +16%; 2028 +14%; 2029 +12%; 2030-31 +10%; China/Europe growth beats; net yield +8%; OCF margin 42%; market awards premium multiple; 2028 FCF >$4B. | **$142** (+53%) |\n| **Total** | **100%** | | **Expected: $102** |\n\n**Step 4-3. Bayesian Update**\n\n- Prior (base rate): 60% probability of sustained double-digit growth.\n- Q1 2026 evidence: bookings 92%/38%, net yield +9.5%, EBITDA +43.9%.\n- Likelihood ratio for success: ~1.3x. \n- **Posterior base-case probability: 60%** (Bayesian posterior, clamped to 60% due to 1.5x base-rate deviation rule).\n\n**Step 4-4/5. Expected Price & Margin of Safety**\n\n- Expected Price = 0.20\u00d7$55 + 0.50\u00d7$98 + 0.30\u00d7$142 = **$102**\n- 50% CI: **$75 \u2013 $115**\n- 80% CI: **$55 \u2013 $142**\n- MoS = ($98 \u2212 $92.79) / $98 = **+5.4%** \u2192 **Limited** (using central IV)\n- MoS vs expected value = ($102 \u2212 $92.79) / $102 = **+9.0%** \u2192 **Limited-to-Adequate**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\n**Step 4.5-1/2.**\n\n- Trailing P/E 36x implies market capitalizes ~36 years of current EPS \u2014 a long-duration growth view.\n- Implied market horizon: 7-10 years of strong growth (reverse-DCF requires above-consensus performance to justify $92.79).\n- My horizon: 3-5 years.\n- **Spread: 2-5 years market vs mine \u2192 negative-to-neutral. No arbitrage edge; the market is already pricing a long runway.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\n**Step 5-1. Conviction Score (Total 15)**\n\n| Component | Score (0-3) | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1. Business Quality | **3** | Wide moat, 60% repeat rate, 23% margins, negative WC model |\n| 2. Valuation Attractiveness | **1** | Only +5-9% MoS; 36x trailing P/E; market pricing central case |\n| 3. Macro Alignment | **1** | Neutral-to-headwind; high rates, late-cycle risk |\n| 4. Thesis Clarity | **3** | Bookings %, net yield, capex, occupancy \u2014 all publicly and quarterly visible |\n| 5. Execution Traceability | **2** | Beat Q2 2026 EPS by $0.36; raised? [Estimate]; but CEO transition (Hagen\u2192Talactac) unproven |\n| **Total** | **10/15 \u2014 High Conviction** | |\n\n**Step 5-2. Conviction Decay:** -1 point every 6 months unless new confirming evidence (booking rates, margin expansion).\n\n**Step 5-3. Sizing Guidelines (before adjustments):** High conviction \u2192 5-8% max weight. Applied cap: **6%**.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\n| Metric | Value | Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Short % of float | 3.52% [Actual \u2014 vendor] | Low; not a squeeze candidate |\n| Days to cover | 3.6 [Actual] | Low |\n| Short MoM change | **-17.3%** [Actual] | Shorts covering \u2014 mildly bullish |\n| Institutional ownership | **101.74%** [Actual] | Extremely high; ETF/passive-driven; adds crash-correlation risk |\n| Insider ownership | 0.79% [Actual] | Low; founder likely holds via entities [Unconfirmed] |\n| Analyst targets | Mean $107.4 / Median $109 / High $138 [Actual \u2014 vendor] | Consensus implies ~16% upside; not extreme greed |\n| 52-week range | $56.06\u2013$110.09 | Near high end \u2014 positive momentum, limited reward/risk skew |\n| Crowding Risk Score | **11/15** | High institutional + near 52-wk high + luxury durables = vulnerable to de-risking |\n\n**Crowding Adjustment: -1%p from position size.**\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\n- VIK is a cyclical-quality growth position. It fits portfolios deficient in consumer-discretionary exposure, or as an inflation-hedged luxury proxy (heritage travelers, prepaid model).\n- It DOES NOT fit: value-oriented portfolios, recession-hedged portfolios, or portfolios already overweight travel/leisure/cruise.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\n- Payoff: Bull +$49 average win (to $142 from $93), Bear -$38 average loss (to $55), Base \u2248 0.\n- Expected edge: 0.30\u00d749 \u2212 0.20\u00d738 = $14.7 \u2212 $7.6 = **+$7.1 per share** (~7.6% edge).\n- Odds: 49/38 = 1.29:1.\n- Full Kelly = edge / payoff odds \u2248 7.6% / 1.29 \u2248 5.9%.\n- **Half-Kelly = 2.9% \u2248 3%.**\n- After crowding adjustment (-1%p) \u2192 **2-3% initial allocation.** This is below the 4-5% conviction-based cap, so Kelly binds.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\n**Key Risks:**\n1. **Recession/Affluent spending pullback** \u2014 high beta 1.5; 2027 only 38% booked provides little cover if cancellation waves hit.\n2. **Industry overcapacity** \u2014 64-ship order book; yield compression risk starting 2028-29.\n3. **Key-person transition** \u2014 Torstein Hagen \u2192 Chair; CEO Talactac and CFO Banh new to roles; first full cycle unproven.\n4. **Capital misallocation** \u2014 capex $2.9B committed 2026-27 (gross); any cost overruns or delayed ships compress FCF.\n5. **Geopolitical** \u2014 Egypt/Nile, Vietnam, China routes carry regional disruption risk.\n\n**Key Catalysts:**\n1. **Q2 2026 earnings (8/19/2026)** \u2014 reported EPS $1.31 vs consensus $0.95 [Estimate \u2014 Investing.com], beat. Positive catalyst confirmed.\n2. **2027 wave season bookings** \u2014 Q3/Q4 2026 data will confirm whether 38% booking and +12% rates hold.\n3. **Viking Libra delivery** \u2014 world's first hydrogen-powered cruise ship (2027?) \u2014 brand differentiation.\n4. **China outbound growth** \u2014 Viking Yidun (delivered [Actual]) targets Chinese travelers in Europe; multi-year revenue runway.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\n- **Common bear triggers across a portfolio:** US recession, Fed tightening/LT yields above 5%, oil shock, US dollar surge. VIK is sensitive to all four.\n- **Tail correlation:** In 2020 and 2022, VIK-style assets (travel/leisure) dropped 60-80% in 3-6 months. Historical correlation with SPX during stress: ~0.8 [Estimate].\n- **CVaR (5% tail):** Expected VIK loss in severe recession = **-55%** (from $92.79 \u2192 ~$42). At 3% weight: portfolio impact \u22121.7%.\n- **Mitigation:** Size cap 3-4%; pair with staples/utilities; avoid holding alongside airline/cruise/hotel names.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n\n**Thesis KPIs (from Tornado):** Revenue path, OCF margin, WACC/rates.\n\n| KPI | Threshold | Action if breached |\n|---|---|---|\n| 2027 advanced bookings | \u226550% booked by Q4 2026 (from 38%) | Maintain position; add later |\n| Net yield growth | \u2265 +4% YoY every quarter | Alert; if <0% for 2 quarters \u2192 thesis break, re-evaluate |\n| Occupancy | \u2265 92% (cruise line average) | Below 88% \u2192 cut position 1% |\n| 2026 capex | \u2264 $1.9B committed | Overrun >10% \u2192 revise IV down 5% |\n| Net debt | \u2264 $2.5B | Above $3.5B \u2192 risk of covenant/dilution \u2192 sell |\n| SG&A ratio | \u2264 16.5% of revenue | Above 18% \u2192 marketing inefficiency (CAC risk) |\n| 2028 fleet | \u2264 2 ships ordered | More than 2 \u2192 oversupply self-inflicted \u2192 reduce |\n\n**Step 7-4. Pre-Mortem \u2014 \"Why did VIK fail by 2031?\"**\n\n1. **Recession 2027** \u2192 2027 bookings collapse; deferred revenue unwinds; FCF negative for two years; stock \u2192 $35-40. Early warning: weekly cancellation rates, Michigan consumer sentiment < 60.\n2. **Oversupply war 2028-29** \u2192 competitors discount; VIK forced to match \u2192 net yield -8%; margins compress to 30% OCF. Early warning: industry yield reports, RCL/CCL pricing commentary.\n3. **Leadership execution gap** \u2192 CEO transition stumbles; Egypt/China expansions delayed; cost overruns on hydrogen ships. Early warning: press releases on delivery delays, CFO guidance misses.\n\n**Step 7-5. Decision Journal:**\n- Date: 2026-08-24 | T0: $92.79 | IV central: $98 | Expected: $102\n- Key assumptions: 2027 +13% rev, OCF margin 40%, WACC 9.8%, capex $1.9B/$1.0B\n- Red team concern: 36x trailing P/E leaves no room for a 2027 miss.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 9. RED TEAM & ADVERSARIAL ANALYSIS\n\n**Step 9-1. Short Thesis:**\n1. At **36x trailing P/E** and **24x EV/EBITDA** (vs peers 10-15x), any yield disappointment causes re-rating to ~20x \u2192 $50s.\n2. **Oversupply 2026-2028**: 64 new ships globally (+122K berths, +6.2% CAGR) \u2014 Viking's 2027 capacity +15% is the largest step in its history, timed exactly at the industry's largest order book in a decade.\n3. **Management transition**: Torstein Hagen is the founder and the product. He is no longer CEO. The new CEO has no prior CEO experience. This is a step-change event, not a continuity event.\n4. **Revenue quality**: 2025 OCF of $2.56B is 2.2x net income \u2014 driven by negative working capital (customer prepayments). When demand rolls, deferred revenue unwinds and cash flows reverse violently (see 2020).\n5. **Leverage overstated**: vendor total debt $5.74B vs our LT+current $5.50B; with $1.9B leases, total liabilities $7.4B vs $3.8B cash \u2014 actual net leverage is higher than headline.\n6. **ROE = 3.0%** (vendor) reveals equity value creation post-IPO is thin; premium valuation rests on future growth that is unproven beyond 2027.\n7. **Fuel exposure**: ocean fleet unhedged; if oil >$100, fuel drag 1-2% of revenue (from 4% of gross margin today).\n\n**Step 9-2. Logic Combat:**\n\n| Dimension | Long | Short |\n|---|---|---|\n| Booking visibility | 92% 2026 / 38% 2027 | Cancellation risk not visible |\n| Valuation | PEG 0.87 vs growth | Absolute multiples 2-3x peers |\n| Moat | 60% repeat, #1 river brand | Competitors draw same affluent demo |\n| Balance sheet | Net debt $1.7B, $4B cash | Leases normalized \u2192 $3.6B net debt |\n| Strength score | 4 | 3 |\n\n**Logic gap: 1.** Below the 2-gap threshold, but close. **Conviction downgrade: -1 step (8/15 effective).**\n\n**Step 9-3. Adversarial Data:** No Hindenburg-style report identified for VIK [Unconfirmed]. S&P Research Update (found via search) affirmed rating but our fetch was blocked; capex figures $1.5B/2026 and $920M/2027 cited in the search snippet \u2014 slightly 